##
   A method inside a `generics` block never monomorphizes, and which way it
   fails depends on how the receiver is spelled.

   This is the quiet one. Written `self: Pair` -- no explicit type arguments --
   the file compiles clean and no method is produced at all: the MIR contains
   `%_U_Pair_i32` and `@run`, and nothing named `Pair_get` or
   `_U_Pair_i32_get`. The monomorphizer skips the generic function rather than
   failing.

   The first symptom therefore appears at the *call site*, and it is about
   types, which points at the wrong thing entirely:
   `Type mismatch between 'Pair<i32>' and 'Pair<T>'`.

   The sibling spelling `self: Pair<T>` fails loudly instead, with
   `Unhandled type 'T'` from the MIR builder. Both are recorded in
   KNOWN_GAPS.md 9.5.

   Generic *structs* are fine; only generic methods are affected.
##
generics <T> {
  struct Pair { a: T, b: T }
  func Pair_get(self: Pair): T { return self.a; }
}

expose func run(): i32 {
  var p = .Pair<i32>{.a = 7i32, .b = 8i32};
  return p.get();
}
